[0m[0m
Bug-for-bug fidelity worth noting, since these all affect output and are deliberately preserved: the SipHash byte loader builds its low half as a C [38;5;10mint[0m, so a byte ≥ 0x80 in [38;5;10md[3][0m sign-extends and floods the upper 32 bits of [38;5;10mdata[0m; [38;5;10mstbds_siphash_bytes[0m xors the seed in twice so it cancels entirely (confirmed in the test output — [38;5;10mhash_bytes[0m returns the same value for every seed); and [38;5;10mhash ^= hash ^ rot(hash)[0m in [38;5;10mstbds_hash_string[0m reduces to a plain rotate. In [38;5;10msh_puts[0m the C passes a 16-byte struct by value to [38;5;10mprintf("%s %d\n", ...)[0m; under the SysV AMD64 ABI that lands [38;5;10m.key[0m and [38;5;10m.value[0m in the two argument registers the format consumes, so I pass those two fields explicitly — output matches.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The asserts are the one intentional omission: [38;5;10mNDEBUG[0m is unset so they are live in the C build, but they are internal invariants that hold for all valid usage, and reproducing an [38;5;10mabort()[0m message with matching file and line numbers is not achievable from Rust. I left them as comments at their original sites. Nothing in [38;5;10mc_src/[0m was modified (mtimes unchanged), and I removed my temp build/verify directories.[0m[0m
